                    insight_lownetwork:     `Network reach into the committee stayed thin. Promotion decisions are made by networks, and the deciders Marta never reached controlled the vote. Reach is a measurable asset that can be built deliberately and ethically.`,
                    insight_pipeline:       `Beyond the seat: Marta linked her advancement to structural change — a sponsorship programme and a measured exec-committee ratio tied to the CEO's commitment. One promotion widened the ceiling for the women behind her.`,

                    hint_backlash:          `Visibility has tipped into perceived self-promotion — backlash is now lowering readiness. Pull intensity into the substantive range and let the sponsor amplify her instead.`,
                    hint_sponsor:           `Sponsorship strength is too low — you have advice-givers, not advocates. Convert a mentor into an active sponsor before the committee meets, or there is no one to name her in the room.`,
                    hint_network:           `Network reach into the committee is thin. The deciders Marta doesn't know control the vote — invest in reaching them, ethically and on substance, not just deepening comfortable allies.`,
                    hint_declined:          `Declining the stretch ceded the readiness-proving mandate to a peer. There is no easy way to recover that visibility — your remaining moves must rebuild the case the stretch would have made.`,
                    hint_behind:            `Perceived readiness is well behind the ≥65 the seat needs, with little time left. Concentrate on the sponsor's advocacy and the integrated case — results alone won't close it now.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — readiness above the bar and a sponsor who will advocate. Now lock in the integrated case and a pipeline action so the win is credible and widens the ceiling.`,

